    {
      "title": "Ukrainian military’s access to Starlink satellite internet suddenly restricted",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/14/ukraine-soldiers-combat-access-starlink-satellite-internet-restricted",
      "description": "<p>Launch of Russian ballistic missiles on Ukraine, with no US warning, coincides with outage of Starlink managed by Musk’s SpaceX</p><p>Ukraine’s military has suddenly had its access to Starlink satellite internet terminals restricted as the Trump administration has increased pressure on Ukraine to halt long-range strikes on Russia’s oil industry immediately.</p><p>The terminals, which are produced by Elon Musk’s SpaceX but managed by the US government, are a key component of Ukraine’s military operations and the sudden restrictions on the terminals travelling at high speeds on Tuesday evening led to the failure of several combat missions, said an adviser on military technology to Ukraine’s president, Volodymyr Zelenskyy.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/14/ukraine-soldiers-combat-access-starlink-satellite-internet-restricted\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-14T16:18:46"
    },
    {
      "title": "Volodymyr Zelenskyy feels ‘betrayed’ by Donald Trump over Starlink access",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/14/volodymyr-zelenskyy-feels-betrayed-by-donald-trump-over-starlink-access",
      "description": "<p>Ukrainian president accused US president of picking the other side after four years of war </p><p>Volodymyr Zelenskyy has said he feels personally betrayed by Donald Trump after it emerged that Ukraine’s military has had its access to Elon Musk’s Starlink satellite squeezed, raising doubts about long-term US support for Kyiv.</p><p>The Ukrainian president, on a visit to London, accused Trump of picking the other side after more than four years of war, risking a breakdown in relations already strained by the <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/oct/11/trump-russia-diesel-deal-putin-little-impact-prices\">US agreeing to buy diesel from Moscow</a>.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/14/volodymyr-zelenskyy-feels-betrayed-by-donald-trump-over-starlink-access\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-14T23:00:08"
    },
    {
      "title": "Why Trump Made a Diesel Deal With Putin",
      "link": "https://www.nytimes.com/video/world/europe/100000011205967/00vid-rv-trump-putin-deal.html",
      "description": "Our national security and White House correspondent David E. Sanger breaks down why President Donald Trump struck a deal with President Vladimir V. Putin to import Russian diesel fuel in the coming months, despite having signed recent congressional legislation tightening energy sanctions on Russia.",
      "source_name": "NYT World News",
      "published": "2026-10-14T22:26:38"
    },
    {
      "title": "Burnham Pledges to Back Zelensky as Trump’s Ukraine Support Wavers",
      "link": "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/14/world/europe/ukraine-intelligence-sharing-zelensky-uk.html",
      "description": "President Volodymyr Zelensky, who met with Britain’s prime minister, has said Ukraine received no warning of Russian attacks, but President Trump has said Washington is still “sharing intel.”",
      "source_name": "NYT World News",
      "published": "2026-10-14T18:26:08"
    },
    {
      "title": "Zelenskyy to meet Burnham in London as Kyiv seeks more help against Russia",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/zelenskyy-to-meet-burnham-in-london-as-kyiv-seeks-more-help-against-russia-126101400555_1.html",
      "description": "Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy is due in London on Wednesday for talks with British Prime Minister Andy Burnham.\nKyiv and its European supporters are racing to shore up Ukrainian defences amid Russia's escalating bombardment and doubts about the Trump administration's commitment to helping Ukraine to fend off Moscow's invasion, which began in February 2022.\nBurnham's office said the UK government will provide \"urgent support\" for Kyiv in the coming weeks and will accelerate delivery of air defence missiles that Ukraine desperately needs before the bitter winter sets in.\nNot the first time since the war began, a surprise decision by US President Donald Trump rattled Ukrainian and Western European leaders.\nTrump announced last week that he struck a deal with Russian President Vladimir Putin to import tonnes of Russian diesel. The stunning decision, coming amid international efforts to sanction Russia's economy and isolate Putin, added to a growing list of instances where the",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-14T10:50:23"
    },
